## Rank-Recursive Transposition Sampler Direction

A separate candidate direction applies specifically to the all-transpositions
model.  Generalize the recursive interface to endpoint fibers

```text
Omega(N,L;g) = { (t_1,...,t_L) : t_i transpositions in S_N,
                                  t_1 ... t_L = g }.
```

The top-level identity sampler is still `Sample(N,L,e)`.  Arbitrary endpoints
are required internally when splitting `S_N` along the point stabilizer
`S_(N-1)`.  If

```text
C_N = sum_(t transposition in S_N) t,
J_N = sum_(i<N) (i N),
```

then `C_N=C_(N-1)+J_N` and the two summands commute.  Stable-sorting old and
star transpositions gives the positive exact disintegration

```text
[g] C_N^L
 = sum_k binom(L,k) sum_(h in S_(N-1))
     [h] C_(N-1)^(L-k) [h^(-1)g] J_N^k.
```

This is an exact recursive counting identity, but not yet an efficient
sampler.  The unresolved primitives are sampling the weighted branch `(k,h)`,
sampling prescribed-endpoint star factorizations, and closing the
arbitrary-endpoint recursion without enumerating a superpolynomial state
space.

The naive lift that inserts only adjacent star pairs occupies the special
`h=e` branch.  Global conjugation removes the name of the distinguished label
but does not correct that branch bias.  Subsequent Hurwitz moves turn it into
a structured warm start and therefore require a start-specific mixing theorem.

The consolidated derivation, relation to earlier long-run findings, and a
proposed focused goal run are in
`notes/rank-recursive-identity-bridge-sampling.md`.  This direction does not
replace the still-unresolved local heat-bath goal in `GOAL_SPEC.md`.


